From a8e13cdc9c4ba00ae682b17f1cf7cbd54c9fe2fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <ci@rdna-boosts.invalid>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 11:59:13 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 17/18] rdna-boosts: block 17: the two-arm server -- an opt-in
 prefill arm and a decode arm co-owning one KV

An opt-in llama-server mode with TWO llama_contexts on one model: a
prefill arm (the main loop, ctx_tgt) and a decode arm (ctx_dec, its own
thread) that co-own the prefill arm's KV, so a decoder keeps producing
tokens while another slot's prompt is being prefilled in a SHARED step.
A slot crosses from one arm to the other only at a request boundary.

  --prefill-decode-arms           (env LLAMA_ARG_PREFILL_DECODE_ARMS=1)
  --no-prefill-decode-arms
  --prefill-decode-arms-mode      off | on | auto        (default: off)

The delivery default is OFF: a run that does not ask for the pair
allocates, reserves and logs exactly what the shipped single-context
server does.  `auto` is an explicit request that falls back to the
single-context server (with a message) instead of refusing to start when
the shape cannot carry the pair; `off` and `on` are strict.

What it is made of:

* the second context: llama_context_params::ctx_other_share_memory, the
  same-model ctx_other gate, and llama_kv_cache::other_coowner (a second
  context OWNS cells in the shared map; a mirror must skip the mutable
  paths, because reading them returns garbage);
* a SHARED VIEW of the KV for every memory module the pair can reach --
  plain KV, plain hybrid, hybrid_idx (attention KV + recurrent state +
  lightning indexer KV) and iswa (sliding-window, gemma4).  A module that
  does not implement the view leaves the decode arm on an EMPTY cache
  while the server prints "KV shared", which is silent garbage on a long
  prompt; create_memory() now warns when a module implements no shared
  view, and that line is load-bearing;
* ONE allocation owner for the co-owner pair (struct llama_kv_alloc,
  per-ubatch reserve/publish/release, llama_memory_alloc_sync() as the
  request-boundary handshake, called on both arms and asserted clean);
  LLAMA_KV_ALLOC_SHARED=0 is the A/B control;
* the recurrent-state HANDOVER, device to device
  (llama_memory_recurrent::seq_state_take(), one batched copy of a
  sequence's recurrent rows + cell metadata), plus the REVERSE handover
  at the return so a finished conversation stays resident and the next
  request on that slot reuses its prefix at single-context parity;
  LLAMA_STATE_SEQ_FLAGS_RECR_ONLY is the serialised control and
  LLAMA_RS_FORCE_SERIALISED forces it;
* the indexer pool is laid out from CELLS and is per context, so a
  restore or a handover re-derives it (otherwise a cache_prompt repeat
  cannot see the prefix it just reused);
* the declared second-context graph reserve: the decode context declares
  its reserve BEFORE the arena claims the space (the aux path,
  MOE_EXPERT_CACHE_AUX_RESERVE_MIB; a manual GGML_CUDA_SLAB_RESERVE_MIB
  still wins), and it is never charged when a speculator is configured
  (the pair refuses a speculator, so the reserve would be pure waste);
* the -sm tensor safety work the pair needs: the take's null-buffer
  views (a keys-only cache's shared layer has no v), a serialised
  state-move fallback for a buffer with no device copy, and a
  thread_local 4-slot meta view selector so the two arms stop racing.

Refusals and deprecations, all by design: the pair refuses --spec-type
(speculative decoding with the arms is a follow-up campaign), mtmd, TTS,
realtime, embeddings/rerank/decision, LoRA, --sleep-idle and backend
sampling; -np 1 warns and stays single-context.  -ib and
LLAMA_SERVER_PREFILL_DUTY are reported DEPRECATED and inert whenever the
pair runs -- both exist to bound a decoder's stall inside a SHARED step,
which this path removes; an explicit --prefill-decode-arms /
--no-prefill-decode-arms still overrides the mode and is honoured
strictly.

Knobs (diagnostic unless noted): LLAMA_KV_ALLOC_SHARED (A/B kill
switch, default 1), LLAMA_RS_FORCE_SERIALISED (forces the serialised
state move), LLAMA_RS_DEBUG_TAKE / LLAMA_RS_DEBUG_CELLS /
LLAMA_KV_DEBUG_CELLS / GGML_META_DEBUG_SET / LLAMA_RS_VERIFY_TAKE
(compact censuses, inert by default), and the pair's use of
MOE_EXPERT_CACHE_AUX_RESERVE_MIB.  ENVIRONMENT.md is the catalogue.

Measured (numbers of record: WORKLOG.md, wip/prefill-decode-arms/):
a decoder keeps getting tokens every ~47-49 ms (p95 64-78 ms) through a
2-5 s prefill, where the single-context server stalls 300-880 ms, at a
prefill cost of -21..-24 %; the pair's text is byte-identical to the
single-context server's on 16/16 model x split x GPU-count shapes and
the prefill-logit KLD against the default path is 0.000000 / 100 %.

The three generated documents (tools/{cli,completion,server}/README.md) are
regenerated by llama-gen-docs for the three new options, as every block that
touches common/arg.cpp owes (r329).

Two report-only defects from the applied set, both fixed here rather
than in a new block (issues #353, #354):

* #353 -- `ggml_cuda_pool_peer_copy_ok()` was defined only inside
  `#if defined(GGML_USE_VMM)` but is declared in ggml-cuda-vmm.h and
  called unconditionally by `ggml_cuda_peer_copy_ok()` in
  ggml-cuda-slab.cu.  A `GGML_HIP_NO_VMM=ON` build (the HIP DEFAULT, so
  every stock packager build) links libggml-hip.so -- a shared library
  may carry undefined symbols -- and then fails every executable that
  links it with `undefined reference to ggml_cuda_pool_peer_copy_ok`
  (reported on v18-919794500-r349, also r347/r348; gfx1103, ROCm 7.16,
  GCC 15.2).  This repo's own build sets GGML_HIP_NO_VMM=OFF, which is
  why the ladder never saw it.  Without VMM there is no pool and so no
  pool range, so the no-VMM definition answers "fine" -- the same
  answer the slab gives for a destination outside every range -- and it
  is now defined unconditionally instead of inside the VMM arm.  The
  NVIDIA `GGML_CUDA_NO_VMM=ON` build had the identical break.
* #354 -- both "never expected" fallbacks in ggml_cuda_host_stream()
  returned ggml_cuda_host_stream(), i.e. an unbounded recursion (a
  stack overflow) instead of the pre-crash-#373 behaviour the comment
  promised.  They now return `cudaStreamPerThread` directly, and a
  failed `cudaStreamCreateWithFlags` CACHES that fallback rather than
  retrying the create on every host copy.

Both are wiring only: the VMM build's definition is unchanged and the
fallback stream is only reachable where the old code recursed, so the
applied default tree measures identically (prefill-logit 0.000000 /
100.000 %, same-seed greedy text unchanged).  Verified with a
`GGML_HIP_NO_VMM=ON` whole-set build (link clean, in-tree test targets
included) beside the normal `GGML_HIP_NO_VMM=OFF` one.
